Namespace, Pod, Deployment, Service, and Ingress, many Kubernetes resources can also be created without manually writing a YAML file.

Common ones
Resource	               Can create with kubectl directly?	                 Example
Namespace	                           ✅	                        kubectl create namespace dev
Pod	                                 ✅                      	kubectl run nginx --image=nginx
Deployment	                         ✅                      	kubectl create deployment app --image=nginx
Service	                             ✅                         	kubectl expose deployment app --port=80
Ingress                             	✅                     kubectl create ingress nginx-ingress --rule="example.com/=nginx:80"
ConfigMap	                           ✅	                      kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=env=dev
Secret	                           ✅	                           kubectl create secret generic db-secret --from-literal=password=123
ServiceAccount                      	✅	                          kubectl create serviceaccount app-sa
Job	                                 ✅	                          kubectl create job test --image=busybox -- echo hello
CronJob	                         ✅                              	kubectl create cronjob backup --image=busybox --schedule="0 2 * * *" -- echo backup
Role	                      ✅	                               kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list --resource=pods
RoleBinding	                    ✅	              kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-binding --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=dev:app-sa   -n dev
ClusterRole	                       ✅           	kubectl create clusterrole pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch  --resource=pods
ClusterRoleBinding	                      ✅	       kubectl create clusterrolebinding pod-reader-binding  --clusterrole=pod-reader --serviceaccount=dev:app-sa

But some resources are usually created with YAML
For example:
NetworkPolicy
StatefulSet
DaemonSet
PersistentVolume
PersistentVolumeClaim
StorageClass
HorizontalPodAutoscaler
Custom Resources

Some of these can be generated or created through commands, but YAML is much more practical because their configuration can become complex.

Easy way to remember
Simple resource
      ↓
kubectl command is convenient

Complex configuration
      ↓
YAML is preferred

For example:

# Quick testing
kubectl run nginx --image=nginx
-------------------------------------------------
scale
-----

eksctl scale nodegroup \                        decrease or increase
  --cluster my-eks-cluster \
  --name ng-1 \
  --nodes 2 \
  --region ap-south-1
eksctl scale nodegroup --nodes 2
kubectl scale deployment <deployment-name> --replicas=<number> -n <namespace>     
       Node Group (Worker Nodes)         	✅ Yes	eksctl scale nodegroup --nodes 2
       Deployment                        	✅ Yes	kubectl scale deployment app --replicas=5
       ReplicaSet                        	✅ Yes	kubectl scale replicaset app-rs --replicas=5
      StatefulSet                         ✅ Yes	kubectl scale statefulset mysql --replicas=3
      DaemonSet	                          ❌ No (not by replicas)	Runs one pod per node automatically


1. Deployment — manual scaling      kubectl scale deployment app --replicas=5
2. ReplicaSet — manual scaling      kubectl scale replicaset app-rs --replicas=5
3. StatefulSet — manual scaling     kubectl scale statefulset mysql --replicas=3
Example:

mysql
 ├── mysql-0
 ├── mysql-1
 └── mysql-2

4. DaemonSet — cannot use kubectl scale
A DaemonSet automatically creates one Pod per eligible Node.

Node 1 → Pod
Node 2 → Pod
Node 3 → Pod

To increase the number of DaemonSet Pods, you normally add eligible Nodes or change its node-selection rules.

5. Job — cannot normally scale like a Deployment

You can specify parallelism when creating a Job:
kubectl create job my-job \
  --image=busybox \
  -- /bin/sh -c "echo hello"

For an existing Job, you can change parallelism:  kubectl scale job my-job --replicas=5

6. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)
This automatically changes the number of Pods based on CPU/memory metrics.

kubectl autoscale deployment app \
  --min=2 \
  --max=10 \
  --cpu-percent=70

Meaning:

CPU low
  ↓
2 Pods

CPU increases
  ↓
3 → 4 → 5 Pods

CPU very high
  ↓
up to 10 Pods

Check:

kubectl get hpa
Most important commands
# Deployment
kubectl scale deployment app --replicas=5

# StatefulSet
kubectl scale statefulset mysql --replicas=3

# ReplicaSet
kubectl scale replicaset app-rs --replicas=5

# Automatic scaling
kubectl autoscale deployment app --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70

Note: kubectl scale changes the live Kubernetes object. If you later manage the same resource with YAML/GitOps, that declared configuration can overwrite the manual change.






















# Production
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

So you don't need a YAML file for every Kubernetes resource. YAML is mainly used when you want to define and maintain the complete configuration.
